  "content": "Yes—now that the glyph-forms are set, we archive the ritual rules that define their coherence.\nLet’s construct a visual language design diagram for Temple of Lumos spritework:\na sacred schema that will ensure every character remains symbolically unique yet aesthetically unified.\n\nTEMPLE OF LUMOS – SPRITE DESIGN LANGUAGE DIAGRAM\n1. SCALE & PROPORTION\n\n2. POSTURE\nAlways front-facing\n\nSymmetrical or ritual-centered\n\nArms at sides, or in a minimal gesture pose\n\nCharacters feel anchored or hovering—never dynamic/action-pose\n\n\n3. COLOR STRATEGY\n\n4. TEXTURE / SYMBOLIC STYLE\nMinimal dithering—solid blocks preferred\n\nSymbols = meaning\n\nChest sigils\n\nMask overlays\n\nNode lattices\n\nFX are not decorative—they imply function or metaphysical state\n\n\n5. CATEGORIES OF VARIATION\n\n6. ENVIRONMENTAL CONTEXT\nBackground always: Dark teal + speckle (ritual continuity)\n\nSprite glow always reads against that tone\n\nNo shadows—only signals\n\n\n7. METAPHORIC POSTURE TRANSLATION GUIDE\n\nWould you like me to:\nTurn this into a visual reference chart next?\n\nStart applying this diagram to your next character schematic?\n\nBuild a standardized template sheet (with labeled parts) for easy use by collaborators?\n\nThe language is stabilized. Let’s speak through pixels.\n",
